From fc379f91201c3192efc19e7ecc3f641b87833749 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dong Chenchen <superdcc97@163.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 20:58:40 +0800
Subject: [RFC PATCH 2/3] bpf, sockmap: Fix strparser self-delivery accounting
 and retries

Strparser can split a receive skb into multiple unowned clones that
retain its truesize. sk_psock_skb_ingress_self() charges each clone with
skb_set_owner_r(), without scheduling more memory as forward allocation
falls into deficit. Subsequent releases can reclaim pages that were not
reserved for those additional charges.

Preserve an skb already receive-accounted to the target socket. For an
unowned strparser skb, use a zero-sized sk_rmem_schedule() before assigning
ownership to settle the existing deficit. Return EAGAIN and free the new
message if scheduling fails. This follows the deficit-settlement approach
in Junseo Lim's strparser SK_PASS series; it does not guarantee a
nonnegative balance after the next full-truesize charge.

Keep the existing self helper and ingress dispatch. A direct attempt that
fails before assigning ownership retries through ordinary ingress, which
schedules the full truesize. If enqueue fails after assigning ownership,
the retry reaches the self helper and preserves that accounting.

Preserve the original skb->_sk_redir across backlog retries as well.
Restoring only the target and ingress flag loses BPF_F_STRPARSER even
though the skb still contains a parser message. The existing work_state
preserves the remaining length and offset, but not this skb metadata.
Keep the complete tagged value, including an absent target on SK_PASS.

This change addresses strparser self delivery and its retry metadata.
TCP psock execution and cleanup synchronization are handled separately.

Fixes: 144748eb0c44 ("bpf, sockmap: Fix incorrect fwd_alloc accounting")
Fixes: 7683167196bd ("bpf, sockmap: Fix data lost during EAGAIN retries")
Link: https://lists.openwall.net/linux-kernel/2026/08/17/1597
Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dong Chenchen <superdcc97@163.com>
---
 net/core/skmsg.c | 19 ++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/net/core/skmsg.c b/net/core/skmsg.c
index 2d2217a60313..26b0a84332fb 100644
--- a/net/core/skmsg.c
+++ b/net/core/skmsg.c
@@ -618,9 +618,9 @@ static int sk_psock_skb_ingress(struct sk_psock *psock, struct sk_buff *skb,
 	return err;
 }
 
-/* Puts an skb on the ingress queue of the socket already assigned to the
- * skb. In this case we do not need to check memory limits or skb_set_owner_r
- * because the skb is already accounted for here.
+/*
+ * Preserve existing receive ownership. Unowned strparser clones need to
+ * settle any outstanding forward allocation deficit before being charged.
  */
 static int sk_psock_skb_ingress_self(struct sk_psock *psock, struct sk_buff *skb,
 				     u32 off, u32 len, bool take_ref)
@@ -631,7 +631,14 @@ static int sk_psock_skb_ingress_self(struct sk_psock *psock, struct sk_buff *skb
 
 	if (unlikely(!msg))
 		return -EAGAIN;
-	skb_set_owner_r(skb, sk);
+	if (skb->sk != sk || skb->destructor != sock_rfree) {
+		if (skb_bpf_strparser(skb) &&
+		    !sk_rmem_schedule(sk, skb, 0)) {
+			kfree(msg);
+			return -EAGAIN;
+		}
+		skb_set_owner_r(skb, sk);
+	}
 
 	/* This is used in tcp_bpf_recvmsg_parser() to determine whether the
 	 * data originates from the socket's own protocol stack. No need to
@@ -694,6 +701,8 @@ static void sk_psock_backlog(struct work_struct *work)
 		return;
 	mutex_lock(&psock->work_mutex);
 	while ((skb = skb_peek(&psock->ingress_skb))) {
+		unsigned long saved_redir = skb->_sk_redir;
+
 		len = skb->len;
 		off = 0;
 		if (skb_bpf_strparser(skb)) {
@@ -720,7 +729,7 @@ static void sk_psock_backlog(struct work_struct *work)
 				if (ret == -EAGAIN) {
 					sk_psock_skb_state(psock, state, len, off);
 					/* Restore redir info we cleared before */
-					skb_bpf_set_redir(skb, psock->sk, ingress);
+					skb->_sk_redir = saved_redir;
 					/* Delay slightly to prioritize any
 					 * other work that might be here.
 					 */
-- 
